WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.942 --> 00:00:18.816
Lottie Ermengarde liked to visit Sara in the attic, but it was risky to go up there.

2
00:00:20.137 --> 00:00:22.339
They were afraid that Miss Minchin would catch them.

3
00:00:24.141 --> 00:00:27.484
So their visits were rare, and Sara was often lonely.

4
00:00:29.356 --> 00:00:33.017
When Sara went outdoors on an errand, she usually felt even worse.

5
00:00:34.357 --> 00:00:36.478
She had to hold onto her hat when the wind blew.

6
00:00:38.939 --> 00:00:41.539
The water soaked through her shoes when it was raining.

7
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:48.201
When she had been Princess Sara, walking with her maid through the streets, people often admired her.

8
00:00:49.262 --> 00:00:51.982
They'd liked her lovely clothes and charming manners.

9
00:00:53.823 --> 00:00:57.984
But Sara had begun to grow very fast, and her clothes no longer fit her well.

10
00:00:59.132 --> 00:01:03.095
She learned quickly that no one on the street looked at shabby, poorly dressed children.

11
00:01:05.257 --> 00:01:08.580
There was only one thing that made Sara happy when she was outdoors.

12
00:01:10.682 --> 00:01:14.224
In the evenings she liked to look in the windows of the houses she passed.

13
00:01:16.546 --> 00:01:19.389
She started to make up stories about the families inside.

14
00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:25.674
She especially liked the Large family, who lived on the same street as Miss Minchin's school.

15
00:01:26.815 --> 00:01:28.096
They were not big people.

16
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.641
She called them the Large family because there were eight children.

17
00:01:34.722 --> 00:01:38.062
The family had friendly-looking parents and many servants.

18
00:01:40.343 --> 00:01:43.444
It seemed that the Large children always had things to do.

19
00:01:44.544 --> 00:01:49.786
They played with one another, and went out for rides in their carriage with their mother and grandmother.

20
00:01:51.226 --> 00:01:51.726
When Mr.

21
00:01:51.766 --> 00:01:54.607
Large came home from work, there was great excitement.

22
00:01:55.267 --> 00:01:58.668
The children ran to meet him, kissing him and dancing around him.

23
00:02:00.368 --> 00:02:04.792
Sometimes they pulled off his overcoat and checked his pockets for coins or small packages.

24
00:02:06.453 --> 00:02:09.435
One evening several Large children were going to a party.

25
00:02:11.117 --> 00:02:15.140
While Sara was returning from an errand, she stopped on the sidewalk to watch them.

26
00:02:17.042 --> 00:02:21.465
Two of the older girls, Janet and Nora, stepped into the family's carriage.

27
00:02:22.606 --> 00:02:26.109
The girls were dressed in white lace dresses with lovely sashes.

28
00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:31.088
Their brother Donald, who was only five, was following them.

29
00:02:32.249 --> 00:02:35.350
Donald had sweet rosy cheeks and curly blond hair.

30
00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:41.792
It was the Christmas season, and the Large children had been hearing many stories about poor children.

31
00:02:43.632 --> 00:02:46.813
In these stories kind people gave money to the children.

32
00:02:48.073 --> 00:02:52.255
That afternoon Donald had been moved to tears by just such a story.

33
00:02:53.535 --> 00:02:55.836
He vowed to give a poor child some coins.

34
00:02:57.812 --> 00:03:00.254
As he crossed the sidewalk, he spotted Sara.

35
00:03:00.815 --> 00:03:04.678
She was wearing a shabby dress and hat, and holding an old basket.

36
00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:07.780
Donald thought she looked hungry.

37
00:03:09.001 --> 00:03:11.483
Sara was hungry, but not for food.

38
00:03:12.964 --> 00:03:15.867
She was hungry for the warm family life that he had.

39
00:03:18.008 --> 00:03:21.431
Donald walked over to the girl with the big eyes and thin face.

40
00:03:21.451 --> 00:03:24.414
"Here, poor little girl."

41
00:03:25.515 --> 00:03:26.936
He took a coin from his pocket.

42
00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:28.280
"Take this."

43
00:03:30.262 --> 00:03:31.702
Sara's face turned red.

44
00:03:32.743 --> 00:03:35.224
For a second she felt that she couldn't take the money.

45
00:03:35.244 --> 00:03:37.706
"Oh, no thank you!"

46
00:03:38.206 --> 00:03:38.706
she said.

47
00:03:38.726 --> 00:03:40.047
"I don't need that."

48
00:03:42.048 --> 00:03:44.589
Sara's voice sounded nothing like a beggar's voice.

49
00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:49.292
Janet and Nora peeked from the carriage to get a better look at the poor girl in the

50
00:03:49.352 --> 00:03:53.834
street. "You must take the coin," Donald insisted.

51
00:03:53.854 --> 00:03:56.336
"You can buy food with it."

52
00:03:57.588 --> 00:03:59.370
He looked so kind and honest.

53
00:04:00.731 --> 00:04:03.213
Sara knew he would be disappointed if she refused.

54
00:04:03.233 --> 00:04:05.755
"Thank you."

55
00:04:06.476 --> 00:04:09.498
Sara tried to smile even though her cheeks were still burning.

56
00:04:09.518 --> 00:04:13.341
"You're a kind, darling boy."

57
00:04:14.763 --> 00:04:17.004
Donald scrambled joyfully into the carriage.

58
00:04:20.087 --> 00:04:22.569
As it drove away, tears came to Sara's eyes.

59
00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:25.832
She knew that she looked odd and shabby.

60
00:04:27.052 --> 00:04:30.094
But until now she hadn't realized she looked like a beggar.

61
00:04:31.656 --> 00:04:35.098
Meanwhile the Large children were talking excitedly about Sara.

62
00:04:35.118 --> 00:04:37.400
"Oh, Donald!"

63
00:04:37.821 --> 00:04:38.681
exclaimed Janet.

64
00:04:38.701 --> 00:04:40.643
"Why did you offer that girl your money?"

65
00:04:40.663 --> 00:04:45.446
"Her face didn't look like a beggar's face," said Nora.

66
00:04:45.466 --> 00:04:48.229
"And she doesn't talk like one."

67
00:04:48.249 --> 00:04:52.232
"Besides, she didn't beg," said Janet.

68
00:04:53.428 --> 00:04:55.309
"I was afraid she might be angry with you.

69
00:04:55.809 --> 00:04:58.670
It makes people angry to be mistaken for beggars when they aren't."

70
00:04:59.891 --> 00:05:01.471
Donald frowned at his sisters.

71
00:05:01.491 --> 00:05:04.653
"She wasn't angry," he said firmly.

72
00:05:04.673 --> 00:05:08.494
"She said I was a kind, darling boy."

73
00:05:10.155 --> 00:05:12.176
Janet and Nora exchanged glances.

74
00:05:12.196 --> 00:05:17.358
"A beggar girl never would have said that," said Nora.

75
00:05:17.378 --> 00:05:23.308
"She'd have said, 'Thank you, little gentleman,' and curtsied."

76
00:05:23.328 --> 00:05:26.931
Sara didn't know it, but the Large children were now very interested in her.

77
00:05:29.934 --> 00:05:33.497
Their faces started to appear at the windows whenever she passed their house.

78
00:05:34.758 --> 00:05:37.300
They held many discussions about her around the fire.

79
00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:42.104
"She's some sort of servant at Miss Minchin's school," said Janet.

80
00:05:42.124 --> 00:05:44.746
"I don't think she belongs to anybody.

81
00:05:44.946 --> 00:05:46.107
I think she's an orphan."

82
00:05:46.127 --> 00:05:51.412
"She's not a beggar, no matter how shabby she looks," said Nora.

83
00:05:52.468 --> 00:05:56.595
And from then on they called Sara "The Little Girl Who Is Not a Beggar."
